#### Decay angular distribution

The J/\(\psi\) polarization can be determined experimentally by measuring the dilepton decay angular distribution. The decay angular distribution can be derived from the J/\(\psi\) production density matrix using basic conservation rules, such as the parity conservation.

The information on the production polarization of the J/\(\psi\) meson is contained in the spin density matrix \(\rho\):

\[\rho=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{11}&\rho_{10}&\rho_{1-1}\\ \rho_{01}&\rho_{00}&\rho_{0-1}\\ \rho_{-11}&\rho_{-10}&\rho_{-1-1}\end{pmatrix} \tag{1.4}\]

The matrix is Hermitian and satisfies \(Tr(\rho)\equiv\rho_{11}+\rho_{00}+\rho_{-1-1}=1\). \(\rho_{00}\) it the longitudinal polarization fraction and (\(\rho_{11}+\rho_{-1-1}\)) is the transverse fraction. Using parity conservation rules: \(\rho_{-1-1}=\rho_{11}\), \(\rho_{-11}=\rho_{1-1}\), \(\rho_{-10}=-\rho_{10}\) and \(\rho_{0-1}=-\rho_{01}\). So there are only four independent real parameters in \(\rho\), and a convenient set is:

\[\rho=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{11}&Re(\rho_{10})+iIm(\rho_{10})&\rho_{1-1}\\ Re(\rho_{10})-iIm(\rho_{10})&1-2\rho_{11}&-Re(\rho_{10})+iIm(\rho_{10})\\ \rho_{1-1}&-Re(\rho_{10})-iIm(\rho_{10})&\rho_{11}\end{pmatrix} \tag{1.5}\]

Let us assume, for a simplicity of the notation, that we have a two-body decay, \(\gamma\longrightarrow\alpha+\beta\). The particle \(\gamma\) is in eigenstate \(j\) of the total angular momentum and eigenstate \(m\) of the magnetic sub-state. Particle \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) have momenta \(\overrightarrow{p}\) and \(-\overrightarrow{p}\) in the \(\gamma\) rest frame, and they helicities are \(\lambda_{\alpha}\) and \(\lambda_{\beta}\). \(z\) and \(x\) axis are in the production plane. The derivation of the angular distribution is based on [61].

The decay state, expanded into angular momentum eigenstates of \(\gamma\) is:

\[|\widehat{p}\lambda_{\alpha}\lambda_{\beta}\rangle=\sum_{j,m}|jm\lambda_{ \alpha}\lambda_{\beta}\rangle\sqrt{\frac{2j+1}{4\pi}}D^{j*}_{m\lambda}(\phi, \theta,\phi), \tag{1.6}\]

where \(\widehat{p}\) is a unity vector in the direction of \(\overrightarrow{p}\), \(\lambda=\lambda_{\alpha}-\lambda_{\beta}\), \(D^{j}_{m\lambda}\) are _Wigner D-matrices_ corresponding to matrix elements \(\langle jm^{{}^{\prime}}|R(\phi,\theta,\psi)|jm\rangle\) of the rotation operator \(R(\phi,\theta,\psi)\). The three Euler angles, \(\phi\), \(\theta\) and \(\psi\) specify the direction of \(\widehat{p}\). Wigner D-matrices written in therms of real-value d-functions:

\[D^{j}_{m,\lambda}(\phi,\theta,\psi)=e^{-im\phi}d^{j}_{m\lambda}(\theta)e^{-i \lambda\psi} \tag{1.7}\]and:

\[d^{j}_{mm^{\prime}}=(-1)^{m-m^{{}^{\prime}}}d^{j}_{mm^{\prime}}=d^{j}_{-m-m^{ \prime}} \tag{1.8}\]

The amplitude of the decay is:

\[A_{m}(\widehat{p},\lambda_{\alpha},\lambda_{\beta})=\langle\widehat{p}\lambda _{\alpha}\lambda_{\beta}|\widehat{U}|jm\rangle, \tag{1.9}\]

where \(\widehat{U}\) is operator invariant under rotations and reflections. Using Eq. 1.6:

\[A_{m}(\widehat{p},\lambda_{\alpha},\lambda_{\beta})=\sqrt{\frac{2j+1}{4\pi}}M( \lambda_{\alpha},\lambda_{\beta})D^{j}_{m\lambda}(\phi,\theta,\psi), \tag{1.10}\]

where \(M(\lambda_{\alpha},\lambda_{\beta})=\langle jm\lambda_{\alpha}\lambda_{\beta}| \widehat{U}|jm\rangle\)

The probability for \(\gamma\longrightarrow\alpha+\beta\) with \(\widehat{p}\) specified by Euler angles \(\phi\), \(\theta\) and \(\psi\) in the \(\gamma\) rest frame:

\[\begin{split}&\frac{d\sigma}{d^{4}qd\Omega}=\frac{2j+1}{4\pi} \sum_{m,m^{\prime}}\sum_{\lambda_{\alpha},\lambda_{\beta}}A_{m}(\widehat{p}, \lambda_{\alpha},\lambda_{\beta})\rho_{mm^{\prime}}A^{*}_{m^{\prime}}( \widehat{p},\lambda_{\alpha},\lambda_{\beta})\\ &=\frac{2j+1}{4\pi}\sum_{m,m^{\prime}}\sum_{\lambda_{\alpha}, \lambda_{\beta}}|M(\lambda_{\alpha},\lambda_{\beta})|^{2}D^{j*}_{m\lambda}( \phi,\theta,\psi)D^{j}_{m^{\prime}\lambda}(\phi,\theta,\psi)\rho_{mm^{\prime} }\end{split} \tag{1.11}\]

\(J/\psi\) is spin-1 particle with \(m=0\), the helicity conservation requires \(\lambda_{\alpha}\neq\lambda_{\beta}\), and for decay leptons, \(\lambda_{\alpha}\) and \(\lambda_{\beta}\) can be either \(\frac{1}{2}\) or \(-\frac{1}{2}\). Also:

\[d^{1}_{11}(\theta)=\frac{1-cos\theta}{2},\ \ d^{1}_{10}(\theta)=\frac{-sin \theta}{\sqrt{2}},\ \ d^{1}_{1-1}(\theta)=\frac{1-cos\theta}{2} \tag{1.12}\]

The full amplitude can be written as:

\[\begin{split}\frac{d\sigma}{d^{4}qd\Omega}=\frac{3}{4\pi}& |M(\frac{1}{2},-\frac{1}{2})|^{2}\{\rho_{11}(1+cos^{2}\theta)+(1-2 \rho_{11})(1-cos^{2}\theta)\\ +& Re(\rho_{10})\sqrt{2}sin(2\theta)cos\phi+\rho_{1-1 }sin^{2}\theta cos(2\phi)\}\end{split} \tag{1.13}\]

The cross-section is parametrized as:

\[\begin{split}\frac{d\sigma}{d^{4}qd\Omega}=\frac{3}{4\pi}& |M(\frac{1}{2},-\frac{1}{2})|^{2}\{W_{T}(1+cos^{2}\theta)+W_{L}(1-cos^{2} \theta)\\ +& W_{\triangle}\sqrt{2}sin(2\theta)cos\phi+W_{ \triangle\triangle}sin^{2}\theta cos(2\phi)\}\end{split} \tag{1.14}\]

\(W_{T}\) and \(W_{L}\) are the transverse and longitudinal components, respectively. \(W_{T}\) corresponds only to the \(\rho_{11}\) and \(\rho_{-1-1}\) elements of the density matrix, and \(W_{L}\) corresponds to the \(\rho_{00}\) element. \(W_{\triangle}\) and \(W_{\triangle\triangle}\) are called the _single-spin flip_ and _double-spin flip_ components, respectively.

