### 5.1 \(cos\)\(\theta\) from the MC simulation

\(cos\theta\) distributions for the final result are obtained by summing up number of \(J/\psi\)'s from the data, in each analysed \(p_{T}\) and \(cos\theta\) bin. Another method of determining \(cos\theta\) distributions is by counting number of \(J/\psi\)'s using the J/\(\psi\) signal from the MC simulation (the lineshape). Since the \(p_{T}\) resolution in the simulation was found to be too small, it was additionally smeared with Gaussian which parameters are: \(\mu\) = 0 and \(\sigma_{A}\) = 0.71 \(p_{T}\), see Sec. 3.4.1.3 for details. Figure 5.1 shows an example of the J/\(\psi\) signal with the additional momentum smearing for two \(cos\theta\) and \(p_{T}\) bins.

In order to estimate a systematic uncertainty on the J/\(\psi\) polarization, \(cos\theta\) distributions are obtained by counting number of \(J/\psi\)'s using the embedding. The best value of the smearing parameter was found to be 0.71 \(\pm\) 0.05 % \(p_{T}\). And polarization parameters \(\lambda_{\theta}\) are extracted using the J/\(\psi\) lineshape with additional \(p_{T}\) smearing in a range 0.66% - 0.76% \(p_{T}\), which takes into account the uncertainty on the smearing parameter. The systematic uncertainty is an average of differences between the \(\lambda_{\theta}\) value obtained from the data and \(\lambda_{\theta}\) values obtained using the embedding, with \(p_{T}\) smearing (0.66, 0.67,..., 0.76)% \(p_{T}\). Figure 5.2 shows \(cos\theta\) distributions obtained using the J/\(\psi\) signal from the embedding with the additional \(p_{T}\) smearing of 0.71% \(p_{T}\).

